[
    {
        "content": "<p>IEEE 754 floats should not be equal in the following snippet.</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"roc\"><pre><span></span><code>main! = |_| {\n    echo!(\n        if (0.1.F32 + 0.2.F32 == 0.3.F32)\n            \"true\\n\"\n        else\n            \"false\\n\",\n    )\n\n    Ok({})\n}\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 629929738,
        "sender_full_name": "notramo",
        "timestamp": 1791396519
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Is it silently a decimal at comptime? Or using epsilon comparison? Neither are documented.</p>",
        "id": 629930002,
        "sender_full_name": "notramo",
        "timestamp": 1791396596
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>There's no way to represent 0.3 perfectly using a F32, so perhaps both 0.1+0.2 and 0.3 end up with the same representation? And even if they don't, this comparison may not run a bit-wise equality check, I think it runs the standard F32 equality test, which might be more tolerant?</p>",
        "id": 629933095,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791397317
    },
    {
        "content": "<blockquote>\n<p>There's no way to represent 0.3 perfectly using a F32, so perhaps both 0.1+0.2 and 0.3 end up with the same representation?</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>No. This equality comparison is a common example that demonstrates the inaccuracies of IEEE 754 floats. It should be false. It's false in Zig, JavaScript, Crystal, and Gleam.</p>",
        "id": 629946880,
        "sender_full_name": "notramo",
        "timestamp": 1791400954
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>That's why it's strange that it is <code>True</code> in Roc.</p>",
        "id": 629947164,
        "sender_full_name": "notramo",
        "timestamp": 1791401017
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Interesting. I just ran this code in the REPL:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"roc\"><pre><span></span><code>» (0.1.F32 + 0.2.F32).to_bits()\n1050253722\n» (0.3.F32).to_bits()\n1050253722\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Same representation, apparently. So if there's a bug, it might be in <code>F32.plus</code> rather than <code>F32.is_eq</code> (or <code>F32.is_float_eq</code>). <strong>Edit</strong>: tests below show that Roc is behaving just like C, Zig, JS, etc.</p>",
        "id": 629959015,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791404233
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For <code>F64</code>, we get this result:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"roc\"><pre><span></span><code>» (0.1.F64 + 0.2.F64).to_bits()\n4599075939470750516\n» (0.3.F64).to_bits()\n4599075939470750515\n» 0.1.F64 + 0.2.F64 == 0.3.F64\nFalse\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 629959512,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791404346
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Note that C agrees with Roc:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"C\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"cp\">#include</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"cpf\">&lt;stdio.h&gt;</span>\n\n<span class=\"kt\">int</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nf\">main</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"F32: 0.1f+0.2f==0.3f -&gt; %d, sum=%.17g</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1f</span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"mf\">0.2f</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.3f</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1f</span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"mf\">0.2f</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"F64: 0.1+0.2==0.3    -&gt; %d, sum=%.17g</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1</span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"mf\">0.2</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.3</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1</span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"mf\">0.2</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"k\">return</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>F32: 0.1f+0.2f==0.3f -&gt; 1, sum=0.30000001192092896\nF64: 0.1+0.2==0.3    -&gt; 0, sum=0.30000000000000004\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 629960275,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791404556
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>The approximations happen to line up perfectly in F32, so 0.1 + 0.2 evaluates to exactly the same bit pattern as 0.3.</p>\n<p>It's kind of weird that other languages would return a different result. At least in the case of Javascript, I'm pretty sure that floats are 64-bit, so that might be why (<strong>edit</strong>: it's 64-bit by default but it does provide ways to force a 32-bit representation, see below). Not sure about zig, crystal and gleam, though (<strong>edit</strong>: Zig and Crystal agree with Roc, and Gleam only supports 64-bit floats, see below).</p>",
        "id": 629960867,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791404690
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Actually, I just tested in Zig, it also agrees with Roc:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Zig\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"kr\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">std</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">@import</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"std\"</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n\n<span class=\"kr\">pub</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"k\">fn</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">main</span><span class=\"p\">()</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">void</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">{</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kr\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a32</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">f32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kr\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b32</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">f32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.2</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kr\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c32</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">f32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.3</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"kr\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a64</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">f64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kr\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b64</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">f64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.2</span><span class=\"p\">;</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kr\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c64</span><span class=\"o\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"kt\">f64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.3</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">std</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">debug</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"F32: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; {}, sum={d:.17}</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">.{</span><span class=\"n\">a32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c32</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b32</span><span class=\"p\">});</span>\n<span class=\"w\">    </span><span class=\"n\">std</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">debug</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"n\">print</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s\">\"F64: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; {}, sum={d:.17}</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">.{</span><span class=\"n\">a64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">c64</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">a64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">b64</span><span class=\"p\">});</span>\n<span class=\"p\">}</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>F32: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; true, sum=0.30000000000000000\nF64: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; false, sum=0.30000000000000004\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 629961260,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791404813
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Yeah, Javascript also agrees with Roc:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"JavaScript\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"c1\">// F32 using Math.fround() to force 32-bit precision</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">a32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">Math</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"nx\">fround</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mf\">0.1</span><span class=\"p\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">b32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">Math</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"nx\">fround</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mf\">0.2</span><span class=\"p\">),</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">c32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">Math</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"nx\">fround</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"mf\">0.3</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">sum32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nb\">Math</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"nx\">fround</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"nx\">a32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">b32</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n<span class=\"nx\">console</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"nx\">log</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sb\">`F32: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; </span><span class=\"si\">${</span><span class=\"nx\">sum32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">===</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">c32</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"sb\">, sum=</span><span class=\"si\">${</span><span class=\"nx\">sum32</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"sb\">`</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n\n<span class=\"c1\">// F64 using standard JS Numbers</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">a64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">b64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.2</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">c64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.3</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"kd\">const</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">sum64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">a64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">b64</span><span class=\"p\">;</span>\n<span class=\"nx\">console</span><span class=\"p\">.</span><span class=\"nx\">log</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"sb\">`F64: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; </span><span class=\"si\">${</span><span class=\"nx\">sum64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">===</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"nx\">c64</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"sb\">, sum=</span><span class=\"si\">${</span><span class=\"nx\">sum64</span><span class=\"si\">}</span><span class=\"sb\">`</span><span class=\"p\">);</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>F32: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; true, sum=0.30000001192092896\nF64: 0.1+0.2==0.3 -&gt; false, sum=0.30000000000000004\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 629961600,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791404914
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Crystal agrees with Roc:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\" data-code-language=\"Crystal\"><pre><span></span><code><span class=\"n\">sum32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1_f32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.2_f32</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"F32: 0.1f+0.2f==0.3f -&gt; %d, sum=%.17g</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sum32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.3_f32</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">?</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sum32</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n\n<span class=\"n\">sum64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">=</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.1_f64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">+</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.2_f64</span>\n<span class=\"nb\">printf</span><span class=\"p\">(</span><span class=\"s2\">\"F64: 0.1+0.2==0.3    -&gt; %d, sum=%.17g</span><span class=\"se\">\\n</span><span class=\"s2\">\"</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sum64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">==</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mf\">0.3_f64</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"o\">?</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">1</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"p\">:</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"mi\">0</span><span class=\"p\">,</span><span class=\"w\"> </span><span class=\"n\">sum64</span><span class=\"p\">)</span>\n</code></pre></div>\n<p>Output:</p>\n<div class=\"codehilite\"><pre><span></span><code>F32: 0.1f+0.2f==0.3f -&gt; 1, sum=0.30000001192092896\nF64: 0.1+0.2==0.3    -&gt; 0, sum=0.30000000000000004\n</code></pre></div>",
        "id": 629962337,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791405151
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>As for Gleam, its <code>Float</code> type is strictly 64-bit (as it targets the Erlang BEAM VM or JavaScript).</p>",
        "id": 629962814,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791405280
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I learned something today! <span aria-label=\"smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f604\" role=\"img\" title=\"smile\">:smile:</span></p>",
        "id": 629963448,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791405434
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Thanks for looking into this!</p>",
        "id": 629967177,
        "sender_full_name": "notramo",
        "timestamp": 1791406653
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I checked in Roc with <code>F64</code>, and it's indeed <code>False</code>. The comparison is only <code>True</code> with <code>F32</code> numbers.</p>",
        "id": 629967303,
        "sender_full_name": "notramo",
        "timestamp": 1791406698
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>I thought both <code>F32</code> and <code>F64</code> have imprecision with these values, so I didn't consider that <code>F32</code> vs. <code>F64</code> would make a difference.</p>",
        "id": 629967968,
        "sender_full_name": "notramo",
        "timestamp": 1791406891
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>For any math folks out there, I'm kinda curious if F64 closes the door on this. I suspect there are infinite widths where they're equal and infinite widths where they're off by a bit. But I truly don't know.</p>",
        "id": 630006892,
        "sender_full_name": "Steve Howell",
        "timestamp": 1791423217
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Interesting question. I think you're right.</p>",
        "id": 630015846,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791427627
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1207194\">notramo</span> has marked this topic as resolved.</p>",
        "id": 630078881,
        "sender_full_name": "Notification Bot",
        "timestamp": 1791449401
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1009034\">Steve Howell</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/231634-beginners/topic/.E2.9C.94.20Why.20is.20.600.2E1.20.2B.200.2E2.20.3D.3D.200.2E3.60.20actually.20.60True.60.20in.20Roc.3F/near/630006892\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>For any math folks out there, I'm kinda curious if F64 closes the door on this. I suspect there are infinite widths where they're equal and infinite widths where they're off by a bit. But I truly don't know.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You successfully <a href=\"https://m.xkcd.com/356/\">nerd-sniped</a> me, I just <em>had</em> to research this. <span aria-label=\"sweat smile\" class=\"emoji emoji-1f605\" role=\"img\" title=\"sweat smile\">:sweat_smile:</span>  My conclusion is that <code>0.1+0.2 == 0.3</code> if and only if the number of precision bits is an even number:  F32 has 24 precision bits, which is even, so <code>0.1+0.2 == 0.3</code>. But F64 has 53 bits of precision, which is odd, so <code>0.1+0.2 != 0.3</code>. Indeed, 0.1, 0.2, and 0.3, all end with repeating 0011 in binary notation. Depending on where you truncate this sequence (rounding the result, with ties go toward even), we get perfect equality or an off-by-one in the last bit.</p>",
        "id": 630082859,
        "sender_full_name": "Aurélien Geron",
        "timestamp": 1791450354
    },
    {
        "content": "<p>Roc also has a <a href=\"https://roc-lang.org/docs/main/Num/#Dec\">Dec</a> type. I wasn't quite sure how to ask the repl if this is the type its using, but Dev should be able to represent exact decimal values</p>",
        "id": 630216786,
        "sender_full_name": "Trevor Settles",
        "timestamp": 1791480787
    },
    {
        "content": "<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"734214\">Aurélien Geron</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/231634-beginners/topic/.E2.9C.94.20Why.20is.20.600.2E1.20.2B.200.2E2.20.3D.3D.200.2E3.60.20actually.20.60True.60.20in.20Roc.3F/near/630082859\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p><span class=\"user-mention silent\" data-user-id=\"1009034\">Steve Howell</span> <a href=\"#narrow/channel/231634-beginners/topic/.E2.9C.94.20Why.20is.20.600.2E1.20.2B.200.2E2.20.3D.3D.200.2E3.60.20actually.20.60True.60.20in.20Roc.3F/near/630006892\">said</a>:</p>\n<blockquote>\n<p>For any math folks out there, I'm kinda curious if F64 closes the door on this. I suspect there are infinite widths where they're equal and infinite widths where they're off by a bit. But I truly don't know.</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>You successfully <a href=\"https://m.xkcd.com/356/\">nerd-sniped</a> me</p>\n</blockquote>\n<p>Thank you for looking into this! I wasn't thinking that F64 had 53 bits of precision. I would guess that future architectures like F256 (if there's actually a reason to go that high) would generally have even bits of precision.</p>",
        "id": 630250815,
        "sender_full_name": "Steve Howell",
        "timestamp": 1791488962
    }
]